---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const IP = `INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY

  "Material created on a computer is the
   intellectual property of the creator or
   an organization."

  Ease of access and distribution of digitized
  information raises concerns regarding

    OWNERSHIP        who has it
    VALUE            what it is worth
    USE              what may be done with it`;

const Plagiarism = `PLAGIARISM

  "The use of material created by someone else
   WITHOUT PERMISSION and PRESENTED AS ONE'S OWN
   is plagiarism, and may have legal consequences."

Two conditions. Both matter.`;

const Three = `THREE LEGAL WAYS TO USE SOMEONE ELSE'S WORK

  CREATIVE COMMONS   a public copyright license that
                     enables free distribution of an
                     otherwise copyrighted work

  OPEN SOURCE        programs made freely available,
                     which may be redistributed and
                     modified

  OPEN ACCESS        online research output, free of
                     restrictions on access and of
                     many restrictions on use`;

const Rule = `AND THE RULE THAT COVERS EVERYTHING ELSE

  "The use of material created by someone other
   than you SHOULD ALWAYS BE CITED."

You already followed this. Session 53.`;

const Enabled = `THE PART THAT IS NOT A WARNING

  "Creative Commons, open source, and open access
   have ENABLED BROAD ACCESS to digital information."

These licenses are not loopholes.
They are how a great deal of the useful internet
came to exist.`;

const Raise = `COMPUTING INNOVATIONS THAT RAISE CONCERNS
-- the exam's own examples

  software that allows access to digital media
    downloads and streaming

  algorithms that include bias

  devices that collect and analyze data by
    continuously monitoring activity`;

const Divide = `AND ONE MORE

  "The digital divide raises ETHICAL concerns
   around computing."

Session 71 was about access.
This is the sentence that makes it an ethics topic.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 3.15: Legal and Ethical Concerns</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Rule You Already Followed</h2>

          <p>
            <b>At session 53 you were told to credit any code you did not write.</b> You
            did it, or you did not need to, and either way it was a rule handed to you
            without much of a reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">This session is the reason</b> &mdash; and the three
            licenses that make it possible to legally use other people's work at all.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 3.15</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Legal and Ethical Concerns</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Intellectual Property</p>
              <p>Three Licenses</p>
              <p>Always Cite</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>It Belongs to Somebody</h2>
              <Code code={IP} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Intellectual Property" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Made on a computer, and it is already owned. <b>No filing required.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why Digital Makes It Hard</h2>
              <p>A copy costs nothing and is identical.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Which is exactly why <b>ease of access and distribution</b> is the phrase
                the exam uses.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">The problem is not that copying is wrong. It is that copying is <b>free</b>.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Plagiarism Has Two Conditions</h2>
              <Code code={Plagiarism} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Both Halves" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment"><b>Without permission</b>, and <b>presented as your own</b>.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>So Credit Changes Everything</h2>
              <p>Using someone's code <b>with</b> credit is not plagiarism.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Using it <b>without</b> credit is &mdash; even if you were allowed to use it.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b class="text-sn-theme">Permission and attribution are different things.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Three Ways to Use It Legally</h2>
              <Code code={Three} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Named by the Exam" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Distinction Worth Keeping</h2>
              <p><b>Open source</b> is about <b>programs</b> &mdash; free to use, redistribute, and modify.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Open access</b> is about <b>research output</b> &mdash; free to read and largely free to use.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Creative Commons</b> is a <b>license</b> a creator applies to their own work.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Creative Commons Is a Choice</h2>
              <p>Not the absence of copyright. <b>A copyright holder choosing to grant rights.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Used when the creator <i>wants</i> others to share, use, and build on
                the work.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">A teacher publishing lessons hoping somebody improves them.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>And Then the Catch-All</h2>
              <Code code={Rule} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Always" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Not &quot;when required.&quot; <b>Always.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>This Is Not Only a Warning</h2>
              <Code code={Enabled} frame="code" lang="txt" title="What They Built" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Most of the tools you have used this year exist because of one of these three.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Where Concerns Come From</h2>
              <Code code={Raise} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Exam's Examples" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">The second one was last session's whole topic.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Information Can Harm</h2>
              <p>
                <b>&quot;As with any technology or medium, using computing to harm
                individuals or groups of people raises legal and ethical
                concerns.&quot;</b>
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                And computing plays a role in <b>social and political issues</b>, which
                raises concerns of its own.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>And One Sentence That Reaches Back</h2>
              <Code code={Divide} frame="code" lang="txt" title="IOC-1.F.10" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Explaining that material created on a computer is intellectual property</li>
                <li>Stating both conditions that make something plagiarism</li>
                <li>Distinguishing Creative Commons, open source, and open access</li>
                <li>Explaining that material not created by you should always be cited</li>
                <li>Explaining how computing innovations raise legal and ethical concerns</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Intellectual property</dt>
                  <dd>Material created by someone, belonging to its creator or their organization.</dd>
                  <dt>Plagiarism</dt>
                  <dd>Using material created by someone else, without permission, presented as one's own.</dd>
                  <dt>Creative Commons</dt>
                  <dd>A public copyright license enabling free distribution of an otherwise copyrighted work.</dd>
                  <dt>Open source</dt>
                  <dd>Programs made freely available, which may be redistributed and modified.</dd>
                  <dt>Open access</dt>
                  <dd>Online research output free of restrictions on access and of many restrictions on use.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Today's Work</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Is It Plagiarism?</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Plagiarism needs both conditions: without permission, and presented as
            your own.</b> For each, say yes or no and which condition decides it.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>You copy a function from a forum and add a comment naming where it came from</li>
            <li>You copy a function from a forum and say nothing</li>
            <li>You use an open source library and list it in your documentation</li>
            <li>You use an open source library and say nothing</li>
            <li>You paraphrase somebody's explanation in your own words with no credit</li>
            <li>Your teacher gives you starter code and you add a comment saying so</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Item 4 is the one that catches people.</b> The
            license permits the use. Say what is still wrong, and which of the two
            conditions it fails.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Which License?</h3>

          <p><b>Creative Commons</b>, <b>open source</b>, or <b>open access</b>?</p>

          <ol>
            <li>A photograph a creator has licensed for anyone to share and adapt with credit</li>
            <li>A programming library you can download, use, and modify</li>
            <li>A published research paper anybody can read without paying</li>
            <li>A set of teaching materials a teacher published hoping others improve them</li>
            <li>An operating system whose source code anybody can inspect</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>Then, in one sentence each:</b> what kind of <i>thing</i> does each of the
            three apply to? <b>That is the distinction the exam tests.</b>
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Not Only a Warning</h3>

          <p>
            The exam says these three have <b>enabled broad access to digital
            information.</b>
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              <b>Name one thing you used this year</b> that exists because of open source
              or open access.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Describe what would be different</b> if every tool had to be bought
              individually.
            </li>
            <li>
              A creator who applies a Creative Commons license <b>gives up money they
              could have charged.</b> Give one reason somebody would do that.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Now the other side.</b> Sites that failed to protect intellectual
              property have been shut down and fined. Explain why safeguarding IP and
              enabling broad access are <b>both</b> reasonable goals, and where they
              conflict.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 4: Two Concerns, Properly Argued</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Pick two</b> of the exam's own examples: media download and streaming
            software, algorithms that include bias, or devices that continuously monitor
            activity.
          </p>

          <p>For each, in full sentences:</p>

          <ol>
            <li><b>What legal concern</b> does it raise, and for whom?</li>
            <li><b>What ethical concern</b> does it raise, and how is that different from the legal one?</li>
            <li><b>Name a beneficiary.</b> Somebody is better off, or it would not exist.</li>
            <li><b>Who should decide</b> what is allowed here? Not &quot;the government&quot; on its own &mdash; say who and why.</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>Question 2 is the graded one.</b> Legal and ethical are not synonyms &mdash;
            something can be legal and wrong, or illegal and defensible. <b>Your answer
            has to show the gap.</b>
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Who owns material created on a computer?</li>
            <li>Name <b>both</b> conditions for plagiarism.</li>
            <li>What is the difference between open source and open access?</li>
            <li>When should material created by someone else be cited?</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>At session 53 you were told to credit code you did not write. Now that you know why, describe what the rule is actually protecting.</li>
            <li>Something can be legal and still wrong. Give an example from computing, and say what makes it one and not the other.</li>
            <li>&quot;The digital divide raises ethical concerns around computing.&quot; Explain how session 71's topic becomes an ethics question rather than an access question.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>All four tasks in full sentences, plus your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
